  "worked_solution": "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as \n\nFor any natural number $m,$ we have that ${4}^{m}$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\nNow, \"${4}^{m}$ is a multiple of $2$\" means that\n\nThere exists an integer number $k$ such that ${4}^{m}=2k.$\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\nFor any natural number $m,$ there exists an integer number $k$ such that ${4}^{m}=2k.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$\u2200\\,m\\in N,\\,\u2203\\,k\\in Z,\\,{4}^{m}=2k.$",
